post_install() {
  cat <<'EOF'
smcbatt is built by DKMS for every installed kernel 7.3 and newer. Older
kernels still ship applesmc and ACPI SBS, so DKMS skips them on purpose.

The unit is enabled but not started here: the DKMS hook may not have built the
module yet. Start it once, or reboot:
  sudo systemctl start smcbatt.service

The charge limit needs nothing from this package: macsmc-power exposes
charge_control_end_threshold and UPower drives it, so a shell's charge-limit
toggle (Noctalia has one, 80% on/off) works on its own. battlimit is for what
that path cannot do - a value other than 80/100, and applying it at boot:

  battlimit                       # current and configured
  sudo battlimit set 75           # apply and remember in /etc/battlimit.conf
  sudo battlimit off              # back to 100%
  sudo systemctl enable --now battlimit.service   # optional, see below

battlimit.service is installed DISABLED on purpose: enabling it would rewrite
the limit from /etc/battlimit.conf at every boot and undo a toggle you set in
the shell. Enable it only if you want that file to be the authority.

Check it:
  lsmod | grep smcbatt
  upower -i /org/freedesktop/UPower/devices/battery_macsmc_battery
  cat /sys/class/power_supply/macsmc-battery/charge_control_end_threshold
EOF

  if [ -d /run/systemd/system ]; then
    systemctl enable smcbatt.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
  fi
}

post_upgrade() {
  post_install
}

pre_remove() {
  if [ -d /run/systemd/system ]; then
    systemctl disable --now smcbatt.service battlimit.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
  fi
  # Reset the SMC only if this package was the one enforcing the limit: a limit
  # set from a desktop toggle is the user's choice and must survive removal.
  if systemctl is-enabled --quiet battlimit.service 2>/dev/null; then
    if [ -e /sys/class/power_supply/macsmc-battery/charge_control_end_threshold ]; then
      printf '100\n' > /sys/class/power_supply/macsmc-battery/charge_control_end_threshold 2>/dev/null || true
    fi
  fi
}
